asuries the  10-year Treasury yields about two  percent and under that scenario the  enormity of our debt is manageable at  least the interest payments we can't  possibly pay back the principal but we  can at least make those minimum interest  payments but the minute our creditors  figure out that we're in the same  position as Greece or Puerto Rico  they're going to demand higher rate of  interest from us and we can't  hey either Greece is going to have to  basically default you think of course  see Greece can't print euros so if they  they have to default America can print  dollar so we don't have to default in  you know in actuality where we just not  don't pay but what we can do is print  money and pay people with worthless  dollars but that's the same result  because our creditors don't get any  purchasing power and I would argue that  defaulting through inflation which is  what it is when the central bank ends up  monetizing the debt because the  taxpayers you know can't pay it I think  that's actually more damaging to the  economy and to our creditors than a  legitimate restructuring where you  acknowledge your problem and then the  creditors agree to accept less than full  face value and you also have reductions  in government spending  you tell pensioners or people who have  been promised various benefits from  government that they can't get what  they've been promised they're gonna have  to get something less because if you pay  everybody off by printing money  the real loss in purchasing power is  even greater sure as always thank you  very much Peter Schiff the reason TV I'm  Matt Welch
 

